        "end_index": 3952,
        "start_index": 3841,
        "title": "Here\u2019s a look at the annual compensation planning process at Google.",
        "type": "url_citation",
        "url": "https://services.google.com/fh/files/blogs/pay_equity_info_sheet.pdf?utm_source=openai"
       }
      ],
      "text": "## Bottom line\n\n**This is a very good offer. I would negotiate once, but I would not walk away solely over compensation if Google holds firm.**\n\nMy recommendation:\n\n1. **Ask for $271,000 base and $1.55M\u2013$1.60M in RSUs**, while keeping the $200,000 sign-on.\n2. Prioritize **additional equity first**, base second, and sign-on third.\n3. If Google makes any meaningful improvement\u2014especially equity around **$1.5M**\u2014accept, assuming you remain excited about the role.\n4. Even if they make no improvement, the existing offer is financially strong enough that accepting would be reasonable.\n\n## How strong is the offer?\n\n### Annualized view\n\n| Component | Meta current | Google offer |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| Base | $240K | $260K |\n| Target/typical bonus | $36K | $52K |\n| Annualized equity | $250K | $335K |\n| **Recurring annualized TC** | **$526K** | **$647K** |\n| Sign-on | \u2014 | $200K |\n\nOn an annualized basis, Google is offering:\n\n- **$121K more recurring TC**\n- Approximately a **23% increase**\n- A **$200K one-time payment** on top\n\nThe four-year Google package, assuming flat base, target bonus, and unchanged stock price, is approximately:\n\n- Base: $1.04M\n- Bonus: $208K\n- RSUs: $1.34M\n- Sign-on: $200K\n- **Four-year total: $2.788M**\n- **Average: $697K/year**\n\nThat compares with roughly $2.104M over four years if your current $526K Meta compensation remained flat. Obviously, stock movement, raises, performance, and refreshers could substantially change either number.\n\nFor market context, Levels.fyi currently reports median L6 PM compensation in the Bay Area at about **$606K**, so your $647K annualized offer is above that benchmark. Current comparable Google Senior PM listings also show a 20% bonus target and base ranges extending into the high $270Ks. ([levels.fyi](https://www.levels.fyi/companies/google/salaries/product-manager/locations/san-francisco-bay-area?utm_source=openai))\n\n### Your first-year comparison is especially favorable\n\nYour next 12 months at Meta appear to be:\n\n- $240K base\n- $36K bonus\n- $200K vesting RSUs\n- **Approximately $476K**\n\nIf Google\u2019s equity vests evenly at 25% per year:\n\n- $260K base\n- $52K target bonus\n- $335K RSUs\n- $200K sign-on\n- **Approximately $847K in year one**\n\nIf the grant is front-loaded at 38% in year one, year-one compensation would instead be approximately **$1.021M**.\n\nThat is why obtaining the **exact vesting schedule** is essential.\n\nAlso, avoid double-counting your $200K of upcoming Meta vesting. It is part of the $476K you would earn by staying, not a separate $200K loss added on top of your Meta compensation. The Google sign-on effectively recognizes and offsets that forfeiture.\n\n## The main risk: vesting schedule and refreshers\n\nYou need the recruiter to confirm in writing:\n\n1. **Exact vesting percentages by year**\n2. First vest date and subsequent vesting cadence\n3. Whether the $200K sign-on is paid immediately or in installments\n4. Sign-on repayment terms if you leave\n5. Whether your first Google bonus will be prorated\n6. When you first become eligible for an equity refresher\n7. Whether joining late in the calendar year affects refresher eligibility\n8. When your first compensation review occurs\n\nIf the schedule is 38%/32%/20%/10%, your compensation before refreshers would look approximately like this:\n\n| Year | Base + target bonus + initial equity | Including sign-on |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| 1 | $821K | **$1.021M** |\n| 2 | **$741K** | $741K |\n| 3 | **$580K** | $580K |\n| 4 | **$446K** | $446K |\n\nThat year-four decline is the biggest weakness in the offer. Refresh grants are designed to help fill that gap, but you should not treat them as guaranteed. Google\u2019s own description says equity refresh amounts are determined through the annual compensation process based on factors such as performance, location, and role, with manager discretion involved. ([services.google.com](https://services.google.com/fh/files/blogs/pay_equity_info_sheet.pdf?utm_source=openai))\n\nTherefore:\n\n- Treat refreshers as **probable upside**, not guaranteed compensation.\n- Evaluate the initial grant over its entire four-year value.\n- Think carefully about whether you would be comfortable with the back-end compensation if refreshers were disappointing.\n\n## What to ask for\n\n### Recommended opening request\n\nI would ask for:\n\n- **Base: $271,000**\n- **RSUs: $1.60M**\n- **Sign-on: retain $200,000**\n\nYour realistic settlement target would be approximately:\n\n- **$270K\u2013$271K base**\n- **$1.45M\u2013$1.55M RSUs**\n- **$200K sign-on**\n\nAt $271K base and $1.5M equity, recurring annualized compensation would be approximately:\n\n- $271K base\n- $54.2K target bonus\n- $375K annualized equity\n- **$700.2K recurring annualized TC**\n\nThat would make the move especially compelling and would provide somewhat better protection against the later-year equity drop.\n\n### Why this ask is defensible\n\n- Your current compensation is already approximately $526K.\n- You are giving up approximately $200K of near-term Meta vesting.\n- Google is your first choice, making closing you easier if they improve the package.\n- Your offered base is already high, but still **$11K below the posting\u2019s stated maximum**.\n- Your request would increase the overall four-year package by roughly 8%\u201311%, depending on the final equity number\u2014not an extreme counter.\n- Equity is usually the most useful lever because base may be constrained by the published band.\n\nThe $200K sign-on is already unusually strong relative to many reported PM packages, so I would **not lead by asking for more sign-on**. Push first on recurring compensation.\n\n## Suggested script\n\n> Thank you again\u2014I\u2019m genuinely excited about the team and role, and Google is my clear first choice.  \n>  \n> I\u2019ve reviewed the package carefully against my current compensation at Meta and the equity I would be leaving behind. The offer is strong, but given my current recurring compensation, the scope of the L6 role, and the importance of the longer-term compensation after the initial grant becomes more back-loaded, I\u2019d like to ask whether there is flexibility to bring the base to $271,000 and the equity grant to $1.6 million, while keeping the $200,000 sign-on.  \n>  \n> If we can get closer to that package, I would feel very comfortable moving forward. I\u2019d also appreciate confirmation of the exact equity vesting schedule, first vest date, refresher eligibility timing, and sign-on repayment terms.\n\nIf you are prepared to sign at a specific level, make that explicit:\n\n> If you can get the package to $271,000 base and $1.5 million in equity, with the current sign-on, I would be ready to accept.\n\nOnly use that sentence if you actually will accept at those numbers.\n\n## What not to do\n\n- **Do not invent another offer.**\n- Do not say Google must \u201cmatch\u201d $200K of Meta vesting in addition to comparing total forward compensation.\n- Do not make the negotiation adversarial.\n- Do not overfocus on the salary maximum; equity is the larger economic lever.\n- Do not accept verbal descriptions of refreshers as guaranteed.\n- Do not walk merely because Google declines to improve an already strong offer.\n\n## My decision recommendation\n\n**Negotiate, then likely accept.**\n\nThe existing offer is already:\n\n- Well above your near-term Meta compensation\n- Approximately 23% above your stated recurring Meta TC on an annualized basis\n- Above the currently reported Bay Area median for Google L6 PM\n- Accompanied by a sign-on that offsets your upcoming Meta vesting\n\nI would consider walking only if:\n\n- You believe the role or team is materially riskier than your Meta position;\n- The equity vesting/clawback terms contain an unpleasant surprise;\n- You strongly prefer Meta absent a much larger financial premium; or\n- You require compensation materially above $650K after the initial grant and are unwilling to accept refresher uncertainty.\n\nBased on what you have said\u2014Google is your first choice and you are genuinely excited about the job\u2014**walking away from this package would probably be the wrong call. Negotiate professionally, get the mechanics in writing, and be prepared to accept.**",
